{{smaller block|Worshipping God and the Lamb in the temple: God, for his ''benefaction'' in creating all things, and the Lamb, for his ''benefaction'' in redeeming us with his blood.|{{right|''Newton.''}}}}

'''2.''' A benefit conferred; especially, a charitable donation.

{{smaller block|A man of true generosity will study in what manner to render his ''benefaction'' most advantageous.|{{right|''Melmoth'', tr. of Pliny, vii. 18.}}}}

='''Syn. 1.''' Kindness.—'''2.''' Gift, contribution, alms, charity.

'''benefactor''' (ben-ẹ̄-fak′to̤r), ''n.'' [< LL. ''benefactor'', < ''benefacere'', do good to: see ''benefaction''.] '''1.''' Literally, a well-doer; one who does good. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|''Benefactors?'' Well; what ''benefactors'' are they? are they not malefactors?|{{right|''Shak.'', M. for M., ii. 1.}}}}

'''2.''' One who confers a benefit; a kindly helper: as, “the great ''benefactor'' of mankind,” ''Milton'', P. R., iii. 82.

{{smaller block|He is the true ''benefactor'' and alone worthy of Honor who brings comfort where before was wretchedness, who dries the tear of sorrow.|{{right|''Sumner'', True Grandeur of Nations.}}}}

'''3.''' One who makes a benefaction to or endows a charitable or other institution; one who makes a bequest.

'''benefactress''' (ben-ẹ̄-fak′tres), ''n.'' [< ''benefactor'' + ''-ess''.] A female benefactor.

'''benefic''' (bē-nef′ik), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [Formerly ''benefique''; < L. ''beneficus'', < ''bene'', well, + ''facere'', do.] '''I.''' ''a.'' '''1.''' Beneficent. [Rare.]
